{"title":"2006 SMT/General Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Problem_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Problem_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Problem_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Problem_10\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 10</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Problem_11\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 11</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Problem_12\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 12</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Problem_13\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 13</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Problem_14\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 14</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Problem_15\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 15</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Problem_16\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 16</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Problem_17\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 17</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Problem_19\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 19</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#Problem_20\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 20</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-21\"><a href=\"#Problem_21\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">21</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 21</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-22\"><a href=\"#Problem_22\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">22</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 22</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-23\"><a href=\"#Problem_23\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">23</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 23</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-24\"><a href=\"#Problem_24\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">24</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 24</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-25\"><a href=\"#Problem_25\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">25</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 25</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-26\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">26</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>After a cyclist has gone <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of his route, he gets a flat tire. Finishing on foot, he spends twice as long walking as he did riding. How many times as fast does he ride as walk?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A customer enters a supermarket. The probability that the customer buys bread is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/eca5733cbc7868a5feaa39a4215562bad9c8eef7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.60$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" />, the probability that the customer buys milk is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/1/aa10fc328b191089b850fd630e4c04747653da00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" />, and the probability that the customer buys both bread and milk is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/7/6f7e2ca2b7ddabf1b532d10efb65ad6f91a743db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.30$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" />. What is the probability that the customer would buy either bread or milk or both?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>After a typist has written ten letters and had addressed the ten corresponding envelopes, a careless mailing clerk inserted the letters in the envelopes at random, one letter per envelope. What is the probability that <b>exactly</b> nine letters were inserted in the proper envelopes?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 3\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In a certain tournament bracket, a player must be defeated three times to be eliminated. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c6bb7504dc877a30762bdce904c5b94c51fc60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$512$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> contestants enter the tournament, what is the greatest number of games that could be played?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 4 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A geometric series is one where the ratio between each two consecutive terms is constant (ex. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/7/4d776660aac18a573cd96c1c607188f54f04378e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 6, 12, 24,\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" />). The fifth term of a geometric series is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/2933f407c21ceb3f3ec8370f43cd5b939250ffca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />, and the sixth term is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/6/e26ca46a67d19ce2e5b692103305a083a2361fd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />. What is the fourth term?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An alarm clock runs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> minutes slow every hour. It was set right <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/f/d5f12573b88cd8f6782e8cc07a81702df196c36d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> hours ago. Now another clock which is correct shows noon. In how many minutes, to the nearest minute, will the alarm clock show noon?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 6 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_7\">Problem 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An aircraft is equipped with three engines that operate independently. The probability of an engine failure is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/b/72b400bd48ccd220d1f606c24df5e689bb37d243.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$.01$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" />. What is the probability of a successful flight if only one engine is needed for the successful operation of the aircraft?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 7\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_8\">Problem 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s, \"plus\" can be changed to \"times\" without changing the result: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/c/dcc62cfd7d00ba0fff260f46d6dd4199e0a606d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+2=2\\cdot2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"95\" height=\"13\" />. The solution with three numbers is easy too: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/a/c3a81ae97a31c6a76d1e3880db7916401fb26e23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2+3=1\\cdot2\\cdot3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"149\" height=\"14\" />. There are three answers for the five-number case. Which five numbers with this property has the largest sum?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 8\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_9\">Problem 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If to the numerator and denominator of the fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> you add its denominator <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the fraction will double. Find a fraction which will triple when its denominator is added to its numerator and to its denominator and find one that will quadruple.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 9 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_10\">Problem 10</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 10\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the square root of the sum of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/2/d1216dd3855e1bb88b6136b4ce49c58b57747417.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2006$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> positive odd integers?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 10\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_11\">Problem 11</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 11\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An insurance company believes that people can be divided into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> clases: those who are accident prone and those who are not. Their statistics show that an accident prone person will have an accident in a yearly period with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/532f79402cd2d051c7bde5d1c1a6ecce07e3bc2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"13\" />, whereas this probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/2/6a270a7be617f39b92e4821298c4555cffe496c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.2$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> for the other kind. Given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/e/32e5939a7bdd847ec86582225cd653c240b4abce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> of people are accident prone, what is the probability that a new policyholder will have an accident within a year of purchasing a policy?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 11 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_12\">Problem 12</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 12\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the largest prime factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/4/1a4a3a0e015b2bf4695bb3bc6f147075db86ff20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8091$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 12\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_13\">Problem 13</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 13\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37b81fc72ef164f306930fcfb9df18d71a31c87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$123456789=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"132\" height=\"13\" />. Here is the only way to insert <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> pluses and/or minus signs between the digits on the left side to make the equation correct: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/1/8a1601c7d95a5ffab96ee13a38146932c91d7f83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2+3-4+5+6+78+9=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"288\" height=\"14\" />. Do this with only three plus or minus signs. \n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 13\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_14\">Problem 14</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 14\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine the area of the region defined by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/3/083cfd5efa8dfdc58c06c4fd74ac6333a91bed57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2\\le\\pi^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/5/6a583f808332984952b34bf52ecb3c1229b599b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y\\ge\\sin x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"69\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 14\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_15\">Problem 15</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 15\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The odometer of a family car shows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7bf20207b99f6cb33a25a259a5913a2997bfa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15,951$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" /> miles. The driver noticed that this number is palindromic: it reads the same backward as forwards. \"Curious,\" the driver said to himself, \"it will be a long time before that happens again.\" Surprised, he saw his third palindromic reading (not counting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7bf20207b99f6cb33a25a259a5913a2997bfa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15,951$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />) exactly five hours later. How many miles per hour was the car traveling in those <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> hours (assuming speed was constant)?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 15 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_16\">Problem 16</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 16\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/1/e51b70fbadb15bd489c0966118e5bc8a83d94b98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" /> are placed on a circle with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af3a9b410ffb93b9171dbd00ffbda2514fefe7d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle OA_nA_{n+1}=35^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"141\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03c08776f0b4cd68ad80089cc5703172d12a6122.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n\\not=A_{n+2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"17\" /> for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. What is the smallest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c02211144cdb3b9d7ad308b4c47444d3e9c008.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n=A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" />?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 16\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_17\">Problem 17</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=17\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 17\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Car A is traveling <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> miles per hour. Car B is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> mile behind, following at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> miles per hour. A fast fly can move at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/7/78792fe4680a164f537ea72a4f900e4471433c3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> miles per hour. The fly begins on the front bumper of car B, and flies back and forth between the two cars. How many miles will the fly travel before it is crushed in the collision?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 17 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=18\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Alex and Brian take turns shooting free throws until they each shoot twice. Alex and Brian have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/7/bb7e03ccc8985ba9d029ea4add6e3e15ab9a953a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/7/fd7edd3842c40dbc1e54e522917a6e07b43c92e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> chances of making their free throws, respectively. What is the probability that after each free throw they take, Alex has made at least as many free throws as Brian if Brian shoots first?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 18 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_19\">Problem 19</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=19\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 19\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When the celebrated German mathematician Karl Gauss (1777-1855) was nine years old, he was asked to add all the integers from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. He quickly added <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a313bf06349648df7be807bee95b651604ce7a7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$99$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and so on for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> pairs of numbers each adding in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94bb6fb7a3ff722532798b45607025254679e7b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$101$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. His answer was <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/f/24fab0877df929e89e94bbc4c139b5cd8b9b18ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50\\cdot101=5,050$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"126\" height=\"16\" />. Now find the sum of all the digits in the integers from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/7/d0719671cbf653c7ab0101af730760c79f5ec972.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,000,000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" /> (i.e. all the digits in those numbers, not the numbers themselves).\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 19 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_20\">Problem 20</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=20\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 20\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given a random string of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/f/b6f3bccae16d5e31266370fced24edc41538d5da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$33$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> bits (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), how many (they can overlap) occurences of two consecutive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s would you expect? (i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/c/68cd6a09e9ea2290fdd05206fd49c9e31a49902e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100101$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"13\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> occurence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/1/7d1f36514b22551e76b22ba110c61d886f6a420f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0001$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> occurences)\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 20 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_21\">Problem 21</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=21\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 21\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many positive integers less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/5/9f5b442c275b4324f100c365b774e872b6b21f1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2005$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> are relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/8/888c508062c09735bab7ff0e11e7dc80cc7580db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1001$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 21 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_22\">Problem 22</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=22\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 22\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A certain college student had the night of February 23 to work on a chemistry problem set and a math problem set (both due on February 24, 2006). If the student worked on his problem sets in the math library, the probability of him finishing his math problem set that night is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65a767ee7c06c8ff24f9437751f8d906bdb4854.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$95\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> and the probability of him finishing his chemistry problem set that night is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e126f0d41c904d37ba2c332347a24b75d4ac3066.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$75\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />. If the student worked on his problem sets in the the chemistry library, the probability of him finishing his chemistry problem set that night is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/0/870303feff6f491471061a7402eba3e326ba84f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> and the probability of him finishing his math problem set that night is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/7/bb7e03ccc8985ba9d029ea4add6e3e15ab9a953a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" />. Since he had no bicycle, he could only work in one of the libraries on February 23rd. He works in the math library with a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/7/fd7edd3842c40dbc1e54e522917a6e07b43c92e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />. Given that he finished both problem sets that night, what is the probability that he worked on the problem sets in the math library? \n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 22 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_23\">Problem 23</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=23\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 23\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider two mirrors placed at a right angle to each other and two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adfd9ae8a3fac031d3b8b470a52a709a23d4d6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/e/28e24c43456d95b5ab8d4f2f8f85a644e98b7ed3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. Suppose a person standing at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> shines a laser pointer so that it hits both mirrors and then hits a person standing at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> (as shown in the picture). What is the total distance that the light ray travels, in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11daf6550c428f61ab1bd2df723e4e2183af1e0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, x,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />? Assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/5/52582bb8429b5b91ff0f6797a293c1b95fb02411.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y, a,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"49\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> are positive.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/7/677d60070ad46ae8bbee25b39074815c275ae1c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((0,4)--(0,0)--(4,0),linewidth(1)); draw((1,3)--(0,2),MidArrow); draw((0,2)--(2,0),MidArrow); draw((2,0)--(3,1),MidArrow); dot((1,3)); dot((3,1)); label(&quot;$A (x,y)$&quot;, (1,3),NE); label(&quot;$B (a,b)$&quot;, (3,1),NE);[/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 23 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_24\">Problem 24</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=24\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 24\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/9/df90265f47b771d571698809b7b17b40759aac6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$555,555,555,555$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" /> factors into eight distinct prime factors, each with a multiplicity of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. What are the three largest prime factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/9/df90265f47b771d571698809b7b17b40759aac6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$555,555,555,555$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" />?\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 24 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_25\">Problem 25</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=25\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 25\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/c/fac95dd82546821132c3e9adc3ad5e83eaf301ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> denote the set of positive integers that divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/c/2dc793e213be93bee6a3ac4f37196870d77d0d98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(n)=\\sum_{k\\in D(n)}\\frac{1}{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"123\" height=\"49\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/a/93a883ab7c83ed146f35ad75ed4e1e8e51f4a2ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(2006)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" />? Answer with a fraction reduced to lowest terms.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 SMT/General Problems/Problem 25 (page does not exist)\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=26\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Stanford_Mathematics_Tournament\" title=\"Stanford Mathematics Tournament\">Stanford Mathematics Tournament</a>\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Stanford_Mathematics_Tournament_Problems\" title=\"Stanford Mathematics Tournament Problems\">SMT Problems and Solutions</a>\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT\" title=\"2006 SMT\">2006 SMT</a>\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_SMT/General\" title=\"2006 SMT/General\">2006 SMT/General</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018052728\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.054 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.066 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 542/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 15446/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:11174:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018052728 and revision id 47183. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}